# EPISODE 1 — Is It a Good Life?

## COLD OPEN

EXT. CITY INTERSECTION - NIGHT #E01-001#

= Universe B (unrevealed). Mike's second death, seen from a distance: a hook that hides whether the crash was an accident or a choice.

[[Never show Mike inside the car or at the moment of impact. Keep the crash ambiguous (accident vs. choice is an open question). Katie B must NOT appear.]]

A quiet intersection. Wet asphalt reflects the traffic lights.

A sleek, expensive black sedan glides into the intersection.

From the side, headlights. A car slams into it.

Glass bursts. Metal folds. The sedan spins and stops against a light pole.

Silence. A single hubcap rolls across the road and wobbles to a stop.

Distant sirens rise. Red and blue light washes over the wreck as a police cruiser and an ambulance pull in at the edge of frame.

[[Narration is delivered slowly, over the wreck, AFTER the crash and BEFORE the camera rises. Tone: frustrated, yet at peace with death.]]

MIKE (V.O.)
(slow, quiet)
That's me in there.
(long beat)
Last time, I fought it. Every second.
(beat)
This time... I just let go.
(beat)
Not sure if that makes me brave, or the biggest idiot alive.
(beat)
Well. Not alive.
(beat)
This is the second time I've died.
(long beat)
Let me tell you about the people I left behind. Both times.

The CAMERA drifts up, away from the wreck, past the streetlights, into the night sky.

The sky starts to glow, a deep ember blue, as if lit from the inside.

.THE SPIRITUAL DIMENSION #E01-002#

= The spark returns to its soul: the film's mythology, shown before it is ever explained.

[[Visual language TBD in bible/locations/spiritual-dimension.md. Abstract energy, no faces: AI-friendly. Do NOT show where Mike's spark goes next (protects the Universe C twist).]]

A vast living web of light. Countless souls, each a glowing core of blue electricity, all connected by threads of current.

Sparks stream out of every soul and dive down into the dark. Others stream back up and sink into their souls.

One spark races upward through the current, toward its soul.

The spark hits its soul.

BOOM!

> SMASH TO BLACK.

Silence. Two seconds.

A single blue spark flickers in the dark. It flares, and the title burns in, ember blue:

> SPARK <

Beneath it, smaller, in plain white:

> Episode 1: Is It a Good Life? <

Both fade out like a dying ember.

[[Title card ~4 s total: SPARK ~1.5 s alone, then the episode name joins. Sound: faint electric crackle, cut dead into the "Level complete!" jingle.]]

[[Sound bridge: out of the black, a cheerful mobile-game jingle ("Level complete!") leads into Mike at his boring job in Universe A.]]

INT. HALLORAN SYSTEMS - OPEN OFFICE - DAY #E01-003#

= Universe A. Mike, sharp but bored, coasts through a job beneath him. His boss can't fault his work, only his attitude.

[[Opens on an ECU from in front of Mike: his hands and the phone screen. The game is a fictional match-3 (no real game IP). The "Level complete!" jingle from the cold open is this game.]]

A phone screen. A grid of glossy, candy-colored gems. LEVEL COMPLETE! flashes and fades. Next level.

Two thumbs fly across the glass. Swipe. Swipe. A chain of gems explodes in a shower of light.

JEFF (O.S.)
Mike.

The thumbs don't stop.

JEFF (O.S.)
Mike...

Swipe. Three gems line up. Almost...

JEFF (O.S.)
MIKE!

MIKE DAVIS (31) jolts in his chair. Behind him, arms crossed, stands JEFF (50s), his boss, face tight with frustration.

Mike drops the phone face-down on the desk and spins around.

MIKE
Jeff! Hey. What's up?

JEFF
What's up is I've been standing here for three minutes watching you do nothing but play that game.

He points at Mike's monitor. A screensaver drifts lazily across it.

JEFF
Even your computer gave up and went to sleep. When's the last time you touched a keyboard?

MIKE
(deadpan)
Define "touched."

Jeff's eyes narrow. Mike breaks into a grin.

MIKE
Kidding, kidding. I finished ages ago.

JEFF
Finished what?

MIKE
The module. Done since Wednesday.

JEFF
Show me.

Mike taps the mouse. The screensaver vanishes. A test dashboard: a long column of green. ALL TESTS PASSED.

Jeff leans in. Squints. Scrolls. Nothing to complain about.

JEFF
(grudging)
...Fine.
(beat)
You're the best I've got, Mike. That's what drives me crazy. Next time you finish something, tell me. Don't make me find out like this.

MIKE
Sure.

JEFF
And don't forget the presentation for management. The new capabilities.
(already walking away)
Thursday!

MIKE
Thursday.

Jeff is gone. Mike turns back to his desk, picks up the phone, and leans back in his chair, completely at ease. His thumbs start moving again.

[[Hold on Mike playing, a few seconds. We don't see the screen, only his face, lit by it.]]

LIAM (30s), a coworker, leans over the cubicle wall.

LIAM
Lunch?

MIKE
Let's go.

Mike pockets the phone and gets up. The two of them head out together.
